      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "PURPOSE",
          "content": "To evaluate the Frail Elder Pain Management Program (FEPMP) implemented for patients who had hip fracture repairs at a community hospital in 1995. Based on standards of geriatric and pain management practice, the FEPMP included education sessions, preprinted analgesic orders, pain flow sheets, written resources, and clinical support. A Logic Model reflected the program's conceptualization."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Preexperimental pretest and posttest design."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SAMPLE",
          "content": "A random sample of 50 charts of patients aged 75 and older who had hip fracture repairs were audited from each of two periods: the preimplementation period (1994-95) and the postimplementation period (1997-98)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "A chart audit tool was developed, tested, and used. Data were analyzed using descriptive, parametric and nonparametric statistics."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "FINDINGS",
          "content": "Surgeons' prescriptions and nurses' pain management practices improved significantly (p = .0001). Pain assessment modestly improved. Patient outcomes also improved. IMPLICATIONS FOR PRACTICE AND EDUCATION: A programmatic approach to pain management is effective for improving practice and outcomes for frail elders."
        }
      ]},
